Example configuration for pam_ssoossh
=====================================

pam.d/        one commented fragment per service: sudo, su, sshd, login,
              and sudo_local for sudo on macOS. Each is the line (or
              lines) to add to that service's existing file, not a
              replacement for it -- distributions differ in how the rest
              of the stack is spelled, and the comments say where the
              fragment goes on Debian and Ubuntu, RHEL and its rebuilds,
              Alpine, and a Mac.

principals.yaml
              a principals map to start from. Copy to
              /etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml and edit; see
              pam_ssoossh-principals(5) for the format and what the file
              decides.

The trusted CA file, /etc/ssoossh/ca.pub in every example, is the public
key ssoosshd signs with, in authorized_keys(5) format -- see
pam_ssoossh-ca(5). There is no example of it here because its contents
come from your server.

Before editing any pam.d file:

  * open a second root session and keep it open until the new stack has
    been tested from a third;
  * copy the file you are about to change;
  * never put the module in a shared include (common-auth, system-auth,
    base-auth): screen lockers and polkit read those too, and an approval
    prompt nobody can see is a lock-out.

The module goes on one `auth sufficient` line, above the existing password
line, and nowhere else: it implements auth only. `sufficient` is the
documented control -- an approval ends the stack, anything else (denied,
timed out, cancelled, server unreachable) falls through to the password
prompt, so an ssoossh outage is never a lock-out.

Paths in these files are the conventional ones; nothing in the module
fixes them. On macOS the module is named by absolute path, because the
directory OpenPAM looks a bare name up in is protected by System Integrity
Protection; pam.d/sudo_local shows the form.

On RHEL, its rebuilds and Fedora: SELinux
-----------------------------------------

The targeted policy confines some of the processes that run a PAM stack,
and a confined domain has no outbound TCP -- so the module cannot reach
ssoosshd from it. Console login is the case every such host hits:
/bin/login runs as local_login_t, so pam_ssoossh fails at the console
while working from sudo, which is unconfined.

The symptom is a fall-through to the password prompt with:

  pam_ssoossh: could not reach the ssoossh server for the certificate request

preceded by a line saying the connection was refused locally (EACCES).
Install the policy:

  dnf install pam-ssoossh-selinux

That package is standalone, not a subpackage, so nothing pulls it in with
pam-ssoossh -- install and pin it explicitly. It ships its own source as
/usr/share/doc/pam-ssoossh-selinux/pam_ssoossh.te if you would rather
build and review it yourself; see pam_ssoossh(8), section SELINUX, for
that, for which domains are covered, and for what to do when ssoosshd
listens on a port outside the http_port_t set.

Test this before you rely on it. An SELinux denial and a firewall both
surface as EACCES on connect, and `ausearch -m avc -ts recent` is what
tells them apart.
